to the American version, leading to the confusion). The speaker explicitly contrasts the *way* the words are said, which relates to pitch/intonation fluctuation rather than just speed. The American pronunciation is described as "oh, no" (implying a fall), while the British pronunciation is described as "**no**, home, go" (implying a different contour). This choice best captures the difference in intonation/pitch contour described.\n\n4.  **Conclusion:** The speaker attributes the difference to the way Americans pronounce the sequence (implied falling pitch on 'no') versus how the British pronounce it (a different contour). Choice D, relating to pitch fluctuation/direction, is the closest fit to the explanation provided about the difference in saying "no" versus the sequence of words.\n\nAnswer: $\boxed{D}$
